[Music]  teenage climate activist greta thunder  joining a protest in london on friday  demonstrators targeted london's  financial district to call for an end in  fossil fuel investment it comes as a  climate summit kicks off in scotland  tomorrow where world leaders will try to  keep global warming in check  coal made up nearly a third of  planet-warming carbon emissions in 2018  and critics say countries need to cast  it aside at a much faster rate  but in the uk where coal was once king  its use has been plummeting  from northern england roxanna seberry  shows us how it's being done  this used to be the uk's biggest power  plant fueled by coal  now it's one of the last  there's very little coal left here now  jason chipstone a manager at drax says  the plant will stop using coal to  produce electricity next year  two years after that the entire country  is set to follow is this the future of  power yeah we believe so drax has been  transitioning to a different kind of  fuel transported by train here to  northern england would  around 15 of these trains arrive here  each day each one carries enough wood  pellets to power around 800 homes for  one year  we take sawdust as residues from things  like saw mills so all of the other bits  of the tree that people don't generally  want coal is relatively simple to store  you can leave it outside here for years  but biomass has to be kept dry and at  controlled temperatures so drax built  these massive storage domes they stretch  over 200 feet tall and together they  hold over three hundred thousand tons of  wood pellets  biomass now generates around eight  percent of the country's power drax says  it's much cleaner than coal  we believe it's completely  environmentally friendly it's a part of  the energy transition that we have to go  through we just don't know what the real  emissions are energy analyst phil  mcdonald says a switch to large-scale  wood burning isn't necessarily better  than burning coal it's reducing the the  carbon stored in forests and it's also  having all these knock-on biodiversity  impacts we have an alternative which is  very low risk which is using wind and  solar the uk is moving in that direction  coupled with closing down coal plants  the country has cut its carbon emissions  faster than any other major economy in  the past decade  renewable energy is getting cheaper  while the government charges big  polluters a high price for carbon  emissions  how big a deal is this for this country  i think it is a big a big deal because  the industrial revolution began here and  and it was built on on the back of coal  [Music]  coal powered homes trains and factories  helping britain expand its empire  london was enveloped in a 40-mile belt  of fog  but in 1952 the country's coal addiction  spewed smog so thick and toxic in london  it killed thousands the crisis convinced  the country to look for cleaner forms of  fuel can other countries do the same  thing  yeah when we look at places like china  or india that still have a lot of coal  on the system the switch to fully clean  power can happen very quickly  even in the u.s coal plants are shutting  down but it's far too early to count  coal out globally coal use is rebounding  this year and environmentalists are  calling on several countries to ditch  this dirtiest of fossil fuels faster  back at drax jenny stanley says she's  proof that new jobs can be created in  the process  how important is this plant to the  communities around here it's the main  focus really so you've this is i see  this from my bedroom window  the company trained the 25 year old as a  civil engineer who now helps in their  biomass operation as small as it is my  job maybe as small as it is right now it  could be a knock-on effect to ensure  that we  reach that goal of ensuring our planet  sustains and survives  she hopes britain will inspire other  countries to kick their coal habits a  goal that grows more urgent by the day  for cbs saturday morning roxanna seberry  selby england  and it makes a difference i remember  going to london in the 80s and it being  a gray city going there in the 90s and  it was just sunny skies everywhere it's  amazing but the two things you think  about the jobs that how do you replace  those for those who are in the coal  industry and then maybe the one way you  do do some of this is those high price  tags on the carbon emissions you force  incentives of  these businesses and the other issues  involved here in burning the wood  pellets which  people may not have thought about before  right no easy solution there is no easy  solution that is exactly right but at  least they are facing facts

